WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.610
Our school got a big table: attendance and

2
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.390
marks for every class, for a whole year.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.780
Our class asked: where do we even start?

4
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:11.730
We needed a routine.

5
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.180
Today: a five-question routine for

6
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.960
exploring any table of data.

7
00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:20.770
First, what is in it?

8
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.730
Count the rows and columns.

9
00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:26.010
What is one row: a pupil,

10
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.230
a class or a day?

11
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.720
Which columns are numbers, and

12
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.620
which are categories?

13
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.570
Second, the shape.

14
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.790
Draw a histogram of each number column.

15
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.900
Is it a hill, or does it have a long tail?

16
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.310
Third, the centre and the spread.

17
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.760
Give the median and the range for skewed

18
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.280
columns, and the mean and the standard

19
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.080
deviation for symmetric ones.

20
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.900
Fourth, gaps and oddities.

21
00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.940
Look for missing values, outliers and

22
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.040
impossible numbers, like a class with one

23
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.540
hundred and twenty percent attendance.

24
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.500
Fifth, groups and relationships.

25
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.420
Compare boys and girls, morning

26
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:13.960
and afternoon, or villages.

27
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.230
Draw a scatter plot of

28
00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:17.310
attendance and marks.

29
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.640
Then ask the next question.

30
00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:21.370
Quick check!

31
00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.440
One class shows one hundred and

32
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.960
twenty percent attendance.

33
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.260
Which step of the routine catches this?

34
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.040
Gaps and oddities.

35
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.720
Attendance can't pass one hundred

36
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.960
percent, so something went wrong,

37
00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.260
maybe pupils counted twice.

38
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.660
Fix or flag it before you compare classes.

39
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.340
Then write three findings,

40
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:47.660
each with evidence.

41
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.300
For example: attendance is lowest

42
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.520
in the rainy months, with a median

43
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.300
of eighty percent in July.

44
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.320
A finding needs a number and

45
00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.180
where it came from.

46
00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:00.950
Your turn.

47
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.020
Which is the better finding: attendance

48
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.520
is bad, or attendance falls to a median

49
00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:08.960
of eighty percent in July?

50
00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.110
The second one: it has a

51
00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.270
number and a time.

52
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.280
Remember the routine: what is in it,

53
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.740
shape, centre and spread, gaps and

54
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.160
oddities, groups and relationships.

55
00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.230
Then write findings with evidence.

56
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.580
Try it yourself: pick a small

57
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.340
table, like a week of weather, and

58
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:32.620
go through the five questions.

59
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.120
Write three findings with

60
00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.440
numbers, and one next question.
